    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I stain an old concrete patio to look like stone?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, yes, as long as it is structurally sound and the surface will absorb stain. Do the water-drop test; areas where water beads need stripping or etching first. Very old or heavily patched slabs may react unevenly to acid stain, so a water-based stain is often the more predictable choice. Plan joint lines through cracks so they read as gaps between stones."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is acid stain or water-based stain better for a stone look?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Acid stain gives the most natural mottled variation but only comes in earth tones and is less predictable. Water-based stain has a much wider color range, including grays and charcoals for slate, and is easier and safer to apply. Many decorative concrete pros combine them, using acid stain for the base and water-based stain for accents and joints."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I have to saw-cut the joints?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Taping lines before staining and pulling the tape afterward creates gray joints without any cutting. The difference is that saw-cut joints have real depth and cast a shadow, which looks more like stone in raking light. Taped joints are faster, dust-free and reversible, which makes them a good choice for first-timers or smaller patios."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does stained concrete last outdoors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The stain itself is very durable, especially acid stain, which becomes part of the surface. What wears is the sealer protecting it. Most acrylic sealers need a maintenance coat every two to three years outdoors. Keep up with resealing and the color can last for many years; neglect the sealer and traffic areas will gradually wear and fade."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I stain stamped concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Stamped concrete already has a stone texture, and stain adds the color variation that makes it look more natural. Acid stain works on stamped slabs if they are not sealed and have reactive lime in the surface. Most stamped concrete was sealed during installation, so it may need stripping first. Test a small area before committing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What sealer should I use over stained concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A clear acrylic made for decorative or stamped concrete is the usual choice outdoors. Solvent-based acrylics deepen color more and give a richer wet-stone look; water-based acrylics are lower odor and give a softer enhancement. Apply two thin coats, and add anti-slip grit to the final coat on pool decks and steps. Avoid heavy films that can trap moisture."
          }
