        <div id="colors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Picking a Stone Look and Color Scheme
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose the stone you are imitating before choosing colors, because each stone has a characteristic palette and joint pattern. Flagstone is irregular shapes in warm browns, tans and rust, with wide, uneven joints. Slate is darker and cooler, in charcoal, blue-gray and green-gray, often laid in rectangles or random rectangles. Travertine and limestone are pale, warm neutrals in rectangular or French pattern layouts with thin joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan on two to three colors. One is the base that covers the entire slab. The second is an accent used on perhaps a third of the stones to push them darker or warmer. The third, optional, is a highlight or a contrasting hue used sparingly on a few stones. Most convincing projects use colors that are close cousins, not opposites. Too much contrast between stones looks like a checkerboard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Joint color is part of the scheme. Real mortar joints are usually gray or a slightly darker tone than the stone. If you tape off joints before staining, they stay the natural concrete color, which looks like gray mortar. If you saw-cut joints, you can leave them as stained or come back with a darker stain or a thin gray tint so they read as grout.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the house before settling on colors. A warm brown flagstone look pairs well with brick, earth-tone siding and wood decks. Cool slate tones suit gray or white siding, modern landscaping and steel. Sample a few colors on a spare piece of concrete, such as a leftover paver or a section of slab behind a planter, and look at them wet and dry; sealed stain looks closer to the wet version.
          </p>

