        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Tools, Materials and Safety Gear
          </h2>

          <p>
            For prep, you need a concrete cleaner or degreaser, a stiff deck brush, a pressure washer, and possibly an etching product or a concrete stripper if the slab has been sealed. For layout, gather chalk, a chalk line, a long straightedge, and a lot of patience. For the joints, either a 4-inch angle grinder with a diamond blade, or rolls of narrow masking or grout-line tape, depending on the method you choose.
          </p>

          <p>
            For color, have plastic pump sprayers, one per color, with no metal parts if you are using acid stain, since acid corrodes metal and spits rust into the spray. Also gather natural sea sponges, cotton rags, a few chip brushes for the joints, and buckets. For acid stain, add baking soda or household ammonia for neutralizing and a wet vacuum to pick up residue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Safety is not optional with this project. Acid stain requires chemical-resistant gloves, splash goggles, long sleeves, rubber boots, and good ventilation, and you should keep kids and pets well away. Saw-cutting concrete releases crystalline silica dust, which damages lungs; cut wet if your grinder setup allows it, use a dust shroud with a vacuum, and wear a properly fitted respirator rated at least N95, with P100 preferred, plus hearing and eye protection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, choose the sealer before you start, because some sealers work better over certain stains. Most stained exterior concrete is sealed with a solvent- or water-based acrylic, which deepens the color and adds the slight sheen that makes stone look polished. Our guide to the <a href="/best-stamped-concrete-sealer/">best stamped concrete sealer</a> covers decorative-concrete sealers that also suit stained slabs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Project Snapshot</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">28 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum age for new concrete</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/8-1/4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical saw-cut joint depth</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Stain colors for a stone look</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-4 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical DIY timeline</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Preparing the Slab So the Stain Takes Evenly
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the concrete is new, wait. Most stain manufacturers want concrete at least 28 days old so it has cured and dried enough to accept stain evenly. Acid stain in particular depends on the slab chemistry settling down. New concrete cured with a membrane curing compound will need that compound removed, because it blocks stain as effectively as a sealer would.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test for sealers and contaminants with the water-drop test. Sprinkle water across the slab. Where it soaks in within a minute and darkens the concrete, the stain will penetrate. Where it beads or sits on top, something is blocking the pores: old sealer, curing compound, oil, or a hard-troweled surface. Those areas need stripping, degreasing or light etching, or they will show up as pale blotches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean thoroughly. Scrub with a concrete cleaner or degreaser, spot-treat oil and rust, and pressure wash. Pay attention to drip lines from grills and planters, because a stain that is invisible on gray concrete can turn into a dark ring under acid stain. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> covers stubborn grease spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repair what you can, but keep expectations realistic. Fill cracks with a color-matched or flexible concrete crack filler, and accept that patches will stain differently from the original slab, often lighter. Sometimes the smart move is to plan a joint line right through a crack, so it becomes the gap between two stones instead of a flaw in the middle of one. Let the slab dry fully, usually 24 hours, before staining.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="joints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Creating the Stone Joints: Saw-Cut or Taped
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a layout in chalk. For flagstone, draw irregular shapes, mostly four to six sides, ranging from roughly 12 to 30 inches across, with no long straight lines that run through several stones. Avoid four-way intersections; real stone joints usually meet in T shapes or Y shapes. For slate or travertine, snap chalk lines for a running-bond or random-rectangle pattern. Step back often and look from where people will see the patio.
          </p>

          <p>
            To saw-cut, run a diamond blade on an angle grinder along the chalk lines at a shallow depth, roughly an eighth to a quarter of an inch. For irregular flagstone, make the lines slightly wavy and vary the width a little by tilting the grinder. Cut slowly, keep both hands on the grinder, and use wet cutting or a vacuum shroud plus a respirator to control silica dust. Practice on a scrap piece or a hidden corner first.
          </p>

          <p>
            To use tape instead, lay narrow masking tape or purpose-made grout tape along the chalk lines before staining, pressing the edges down firmly so stain cannot creep under. Curve the tape gently for flagstone shapes. After staining, the tape comes up and leaves gray concrete lines that read as mortar. This method is faster, quieter and dust-free, but the joints are flat, so the illusion is a bit weaker in raking light.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever method you use, consider existing control joints. Those straight cuts made when the slab was poured will show through any pattern. The best approach is to incorporate them as joint lines in your layout, which is easy with slate and travertine patterns and harder with flagstone. For flagstone, you can let the control joint pass between stones along most of its length so it disappears into the design.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">LAYOUT RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No two stones of the same size or shape next to each other.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Our eyes catch repetition faster than any other flaw. Mix big and small stones, vary the number of sides, and never let three joints line up in a straight row. If a section looks like a grid, redraw it before you cut.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="color-layers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Applying Color in Layers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the base color across the whole slab. With a pump sprayer, mist the stain on in overlapping circular passes rather than straight lines, which leave visible stripes. With acid stain, scrub it in lightly with a soft brush in random directions to work it into the surface, and expect it to fizz and react. Do not try for perfect coverage. Slight variation in the base coat helps the stone look.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the base has reacted or dried according to the label, start working stone by stone with accent colors. Use a sea sponge or crumpled rag dipped in the accent stain to dab color onto selected stones, focusing more on edges and corners and less on centers. Treat roughly a third of the stones, chosen randomly. Some stones get a heavy accent; others get a light touch.
          </p>

          <p>
            For extra depth, add a third color sparingly. A few stones with a hint of rust or green, or a couple of noticeably darker stones, prevent the patio from looking too even. Some people also add subtle veining on travertine looks with a fine brush, but less is more. If a stone ends up too dark, you usually cannot lighten it, so build color gradually.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the clock and the weather. Stain applied in direct hot sun can dry too fast and leave lap marks, and rain on fresh stain will streak it. Work in the morning or on an overcast day with temperatures within the stain maker's range, typically 50 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit. Keep a wet edge on the base coat and do not stop halfway across the slab for lunch.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
